{
  "description": "The access cases of ADR 0007 §7. Each case is an account, described as /auth/me describes it (its effective permissions in the order of ADR 0007 §1, and whether it holds the admin role), and one authProvider.canAccess question about it: an ra-core resource, an action and the record it is asked for (null for none). `access` is the list of actions that /auth/me sends that account for that resource, in AccessAction order ([] when it sends none), and `expected` is the answer. The Rust test (tests/domain/authz_cases.rs) checks `access` against the server's access table (src/domain/authz.rs, RESOURCE_ACCESS) and `expected` against it and the Rust record-level rules; the admin's Vitest test (admin/src/auth/authz-cases.test.ts) checks `expected` against authProvider.canAccess and its TypeScript mirror of those rules. Every account is invented. Each resource slice adds a case that allows and a case that refuses for its row, and each record-level rule adds cases with a record.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "the retired newsletter screen is no resource, even for the admin role",
      "permissions": [
        "edit posts",
        "publish posts",
        "edit labels",
        "edit authors",
        "edit events",
        "edit canteens",
        "edit colleagues",
        "edit users",
        "edit pages",
        "edit menu",
        "manage media",
        "view logs",
        "edit notices",
        "edit banners",
        "edit site settings",
        "edit diaktoll"
      ],
      "is_admin": true,
      "resource": "newsletter",
      "action": "list",
      "record": null,
      "access": [],
      "expected": false
    },
    {
      "name": "the retired backups screen is no resource, even for the admin role",
      "permissions": [
        "edit posts",
        "publish posts",
        "edit labels",
        "edit authors",
        "edit events",
        "edit canteens",
        "edit colleagues",
        "edit users",
        "edit pages",
        "edit menu",
        "manage media",
